# Adding a unit test

Never add a new test unless Spencer asks for one in that request. No
exceptions, including a test that clears every bar below.

These are not a request for a test: a bug fix, a feature, a ticket, review bot
feedback, or a rule telling you to run the suite. On PR 2571 a Copilot fix got
two tests nobody asked for.

Updating an existing test that the change breaks is fine. Adding a case is not.
If a change looks like it needs coverage, name the case in one line of the
report and do not write it.

## When he asks for one

Write tests whose assertions read as behavior. Prefer "at this voltage the
system trips" over asserting on internals.

### The bar, all four

1. It fails on the pre-change code and passes after. Run it both ways and put
   the result in the report. A test you never saw fail proves nothing.
2. It pins a decision someone could get wrong later: a threshold, membership in
   a state set, an ordering requirement, a firmware fact.
3. Nothing already covers it, including the other cases added in this same
   change.
4. It runs in the suite that runs. In rw-bess that means `test/tests/unit/`.
   `test/tests/eol/` skips without `--serial-number`, so a test there is bench
   coverage, not CI coverage. Say which one you added.

### Never earns a test

- Restating the line under it. "The first sample starts the timer" is the
  implementation read back, and the boundary test already needs that sample.
- A second member of the same set. Standby is the non-obvious one, so Fault is
  not a second test.
- One test per branch when one test covers the behavior.
- Coverage for its own sake.
- Inputs no caller produces.

### Before you show him the diff

List every case you added and the one failure each would catch, in a sentence
each. Two sentences that come out the same are one case, so cut one.

When a change edits the same construct in several files, the unit of coverage
is the file that could have been missed, one case per file, never one case per
assertion you could make about it.

### Cost

Fake the clock, never spin the real one. One test burning the full confirm
window took a file from under a second to 4.88 s.

Prefer extending an existing test over adding a file. Deleting a test you wrote
is not a loss.

## When he asks whether a test is needed

Name the ones to cut and why, do not defend the set.
